//!
//! # Regex
//!
//! Regular expressions with linear-time matching, in the style of RE2: a
//! pattern is parsed into an AST, compiled to a program and run by a Pike VM
//! that simulates the NFA, so matching takes O(text x program) time and never
//! backtracks.
//!
//! This slice holds the parser only. Compiling and matching (`Regex::new`,
//! `isMatch`, `find`, `findAll`, captures, `replaceAll`, `split`) arrive in
//! the next slices.
//!
//! ## Exports
//!
//! | Export                 | Description                                                    |
//! |------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------|
//! | `parseRegex`           | `parseRegex(pattern)`: `Result<RegexAst, RegexError>`          |
//! | `RegexError`           | `column()` (1-based, in Unicode scalars), `message()`, `toString()` |
//! | `RegexAst`             | `nodes`, `root`, `groupCount`                                  |
//! | `RegexNode`            | `EMPTY`, `LITERAL`, `ANY_EXCEPT_NEWLINE`, `CLASS`, `CONCAT`, `ALTERNATE`, `REPEAT`, `GROUP`, `ASSERT` |
//! | `RegexClass`           | Sorted, merged `ranges` and `negated`                          |
//! | `RegexRange`           | Inclusive `start` and `end` scalar values                      |
//! | `RegexRepeat`          | `child`, `min`, `max`, `bounded`, `greedy`                     |
//! | `RegexGroup`           | `child`, `capturing`, `index`, `name`                          |
//! | `RegexAssertion`       | `START`, `END`, `WORD_BOUNDARY`, `NOT_WORD_BOUNDARY`           |
//! | `REGEX_MAX_REPETITION` | The largest count `{n,m}` accepts: 1000                        |
//!
//! ## Syntax
//!
//! | Syntax                            | Meaning                                                  |
//! |-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
//! | `x`                               | The scalar `x`, any UTF-8 scalar                         |
//! | `.`                               | Any scalar except `\n`                                   |
//! | `\n` `\t` `\r` `\f` `\v`          | Control scalars                                          |
//! | `\.` `\*` `\\` ...                | Any escaped ASCII punctuation is itself                  |
//! | `[abc]` `[a-z]` `[^...]`          | Classes, ranges, negation; escapes work inside           |
//! | `\d` `\w` `\s` and `\D` `\W` `\S` | `[0-9]`, `[0-9A-Za-z_]`, `[ \t\n\r\f\v]` and negations (ASCII) |
//! | `^` `$`                           | Start and end of the whole text, not of a line           |
//! | `\b` `\B`                         | ASCII word boundary and its negation                     |
//! | `(x)` `(?:x)` `(?<name>x)`        | Capturing, non-capturing and named groups                |
//! | `x\|y`                            | Alternation, leftmost-first                              |
//! | `x*` `x+` `x?`                    | Zero or more, one or more, zero or one                   |
//! | `x{n}` `x{n,}` `x{n,m}`           | Counted repetition, counts at most 1000                  |
//! | `x*?` `x+?` `x??` `x{n,m}?`       | Lazy forms: prefer fewer repetitions                     |
//! | `(?i)` `(?i:x)`                   | ASCII case-insensitive for the rest of the enclosing group, or inside `x` |
//!
//! Group names are `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` and must be unique. Capturing
//! groups are numbered from 1 in the order of their `(`.
//!
//! The parser is strict where a pattern would otherwise be ambiguous:
//!
//! - `{` always starts a counted repetition; a literal brace is `\{`. A
//!   `{` that is not `{n}`, `{n,}` or `{n,m}` is `invalid repetition`. A
//!   lone `}` or `]` is a literal.
//! - A quantifier after a quantifier (`a**`, `a+*`, `a{2}{3}`) and a
//!   quantifier after `^`, `$`, `\b` or `\B` are `nothing to repeat`; the
//!   `?` of a lazy form is part of its quantifier.
//! - `[]` and `[^]` are `empty class`; a `]` inside a class is `\]`. A `-`
//!   first or last in a class is a literal.
//!
//! ## Errors
//!
//! Every error carries the 1-based column, counted in Unicode scalars, of
//! the part that is wrong: `unclosed group` (at its `(`), `unmatched )`,
//! `nothing to repeat`, `invalid range z-a` (at its `-`), `unclosed class`
//! (at its `[`), `empty class`, `trailing backslash`, `unknown escape \q`,
//! `invalid repetition`, `invalid repetition range`, `repetition count above
//! 1000`, `invalid group name`, `duplicate group name x`, `unknown group
//! flag`, `unsupported: backreference` and `unsupported: lookaround`.
//!
//! Backreferences (`\1`) and lookaround (`(?=`, `(?!`, `(?<=`, `(?<!`) are
//! rejected because they cannot be matched in linear time. Classes are
//! ASCII; Unicode classes are not supported in v1.

import _ from "std::regex::ast";
import _ from "std::regex::parser";

export
  RegexAssertion,
  RegexAst,
  RegexClass,
  RegexError,
  RegexGroup,
  RegexNode,
  RegexRange,
  RegexRepeat,
from "./ast";
export REGEX_MAX_REPETITION, parseRegex from "./parser";
